// RETIRING A HOST IS AN OBLIGATION SOMEBODY DECLARED. IT IS NOT A WIDTH THAT FAILED TO RESOLVE.
//
// srv2 is the specimen and it is the reason this module exists beside gunbc.runner_width_transition
// rather than inside it. A width transition is a host that stays in the fleet at a smaller size: its
// obligation is PRIOR WIDTH MINUS TARGET WIDTH, and the target is a positive number the model still
// answers. srv2 has no such target. `runner_host_deploy_for` answers `RunnerHostDeployRefused` for it
// -- its session denominator is unestablished, so no admitted width can be derived at all -- and
// `runner_width_transition` deliberately carries no row for it, because a transition needs two widths
// and srv2 has one.
//
// SO THE OBLIGATION WAS UNSAYABLE, AND THE TEMPTING REPAIRS ARE BOTH WRONG IN THE SAME DIRECTION.
//
//   1. READ THE CAPACITY REFUSAL AS A DELETION INSTRUCTION. "This host's admitted width resolved to
//      zero" would become "remove every slot on this host". That is the sentinel encoding
//      `runner_host_deploy` already removed once, re-created one layer up and with far worse
//      consequences: a refusal is a statement that the model CANNOT SAY what this host should run,
//      and deriving a removal from it converts ignorance into an instruction. A host whose
//      denominator becomes measurable tomorrow would have had its estate deleted today on the
//      strength of not being understood.
//
//   2. CONSTRUCT A ZERO-WIDTH `RunnerHostDeploy`. This is the same move wearing the operational
//      type, and `runner_host_deploy_of` has no arm that would build it -- the refusal arm carries no
//      `runner_count` field to fill. Reaching the zero would mean minting the row somewhere else,
//      which is the second authority that constructor exists to prevent.
//
// WHAT A RETIREMENT ACTUALLY IS: a declared decision that a named host leaves the runner estate, with
// the population it is leaving behind stated as part of the decision. The population is the OWNED
// identity set -- what srv2 was running -- and it is declared for the same reason
// `RunnerWidthTransition.prior_width` is declared: it cannot be recovered from a model that has
// stopped answering for this host, and re-deriving it from a census would shrink the obligation at
// exactly the moment a member becomes least visible.
//
// THE SPEC TRAVELS WITH THE DECISION, and that is not decoration. The retirement has to name an
// ssh_target to reach the host over, principals to reach it as, and the GitHub app whose
// registrations are part of the estate. Every one of those is on `RunnerHostSpec`, which
// `RunnerHostDeployWidthRefused` already carries precisely so a refused host stays fully named. This
// row consumes that spec rather than re-spelling an address.
// THREE DIFFERENT POPULATIONS WERE CALLED "owned_width", AND ONLY ONE OF THEM WAS EVER ESTABLISHED.
//
// The first version of this row carried `owned_width: 21` and called it srv2's owned population. That
// name asserts captured history -- these identities existed and this host owned them -- and nothing
// in this repository establishes it. What 21 actually is: the slot count of the provisioning template
// srv2 was built from, which is a statement about how far to LOOK, not about what is there.
//
// So the three are separated, because they have different evidence and different consequences:
//
//   SEARCH BOUND      -- how many slot indices this retirement enumerates. Authored, because no
//                        surviving width authority answers for srv2. Being wrong high costs readings;
//                        being wrong low leaves identities outside every gate.
//   CAPTURED OWNED    -- identities a snapshot actually observed. NOT AVAILABLE HERE: this route reads
//                        no GitHub roster and no census, so the retirement carries none and says so.
//                        The field exists so the population can be grounded when the roster read lands
//                        rather than being retro-fitted into the bound.
//   AUTHORIZED SCOPE  -- which identities an operator permits interrupting. Lives in
//                        `gunbc.runner_slot_retirement`'s `InterruptionAuthorization` and is
//                        deliberately not here; permission must never be an input to obligation.
//
// AND THE HARMLESSNESS ARGUMENT THE FIRST VERSION GAVE WAS FALSE. It said an over-stated bound costs
// one reading because a never-created member reads `RetirementComplete` immediately. It does not:
// `retirement_observation_from_readings` sets `replacement_inhibited` from `UnitFileState == "masked"`,
// and a unit that never existed reports blank, not masked. Such an identity therefore reads
// `RetirementPendingDrain` -- replacement is not inhibited -- and blocks the closure forever. The
// invented obligation is not free, and the two ways to "fix" it are both worse than the bound being
// honest: weakening the inhibition test would let a real unmasked slot pass, and masking a unit that
// was never created would be an effect performed to satisfy a number somebody authored.
//
// SO A NEVER-CREATED IDENTITY IS AN EXPLICIT ANSWER rather than an accident of the fold, below.
// THE CAPTURE IS THE ONE IN `gunbc.runner_registration_capture`, NOT A SECOND SHAPE BESIDE IT.
//
// This module carried its own `CaptureStanding` whose members were registration NAMES, and two
// defects followed from that shape rather than from any logic here. The obligation projection mapped
// each captured name through the unit projection, which for a real suffixed name yields
// `actions-runner@srv2-02-1789774588-3693107.service` -- a unit that exists nowhere. And a name list
// cannot carry the org-scoped id the remote half of a retirement is addressed by, so half the subject
// was unrepresentable.
//
// A second capture type beside the real one is also the §3 fork: two answers to "what did the census
// observe", free to disagree. `CaptureCensus` is that authority and this module consumes it.
type HostRetirement sole_constructor {
  retirement_id: NonEmptyStr
  host: HostIdentity
  spec: RunnerHostSpec
  search_bound: Int
  capture: CaptureCensus
  local_population: LocalSlotPopulation
  operator_reason: NonEmptyStr
}

// THE DECLARED RETIREMENTS. `search_bound` is the provisioning template srv2 was built from and is a
// SEARCH RANGE; `captured_owned` is empty because no snapshot has been taken through this route, and
// an empty list here is the honest statement that the population is ungrounded rather than a claim
// that srv2 owns nothing.
data srv2_host_retirement: HostRetirement = HostRetirement {
  retirement_id: "srv2-runner-estate-retirement" as NonEmptyStr,
  host: operator_host_srv2,
  spec: srv2_runner_host_spec,
  search_bound: 21,
  local_population: LocalPopulationUnavailable {
    cause: "no local slot population read has been admitted for srv2: the retirement assessment performs no directory observation of /opt/actions-runner, so which trees exist is UNGROUNDED rather than empty. The operator-authorized census of 2026-09-19T10:34Z observed seven (01-05, 11, 12) against four .runner files; admitting that observation as a declared population is a separate change from modelling the shape it produces",
  },
  capture: CaptureCensusUnavailable {
    cause: "no capture census has been admitted for srv2 in this repository: the retirement assessment holds no GitHub App credential and performs no host capture read, so the population is UNGROUNDED rather than empty. The operator-authorized census of 2026-09-19T10:34Z observed four .runner files; admitting that observation as a declared census is a separate change from modelling the shape it produces",
  },
  operator_reason: "srv2 leaves the runner estate: its session denominator is unestablished, so gunbc.runner_host_deploy refuses to derive any admitted width for it and it carries no width transition. The retirement is declared here as its own decision rather than read off that refusal -- a capacity refusal states that the model cannot say what this host should run, and is not an instruction to delete what it is running. The search bound of 21 is the provisioning template srv2 was built from; it is where to LOOK and is not evidence that 21 slots were created" as NonEmptyStr,
}

data declared_host_retirements: List<HostRetirement> = [srv2_host_retirement]

// A HOST IS RETIRING BECAUSE A ROW SAYS SO, AND FOR NO OTHER REASON.
//
// `HostRetirement` is `sole_constructor`, so this roster is the only population that exists: no
// consumer can mint a retirement from a refusal, a census gap, an empty roster read or an urgency.
// That is the construction that makes the two failure arms in the type's note unwritable rather than
// merely discouraged -- there is nowhere else to build the value they would have to produce.
// A SECOND DECLARED ROW FOR ONE HOST IS AN UNRESOLVED QUESTION, NOT A LIST TO PICK FROM. This value
// is the admission gate for the destructive route, and two rows can differ in search_bound, capture
// and local_population -- so taking the first would decide, silently and by authoring order, which
// obligation the route is about to discharge. This module already refuses exactly that shape for the
// estate obligations ("Selecting one -- first, last, or the convenient one -- is an unresolved
// selection rule wearing a decision's clothes"), and the capture boundary refuses it for bindings;
// the rule was stated twice and then not applied to the gate in front of both.
type HostRetirementStanding
  = HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: HostRetirement }
  | HostRetirementAmbiguous { host: HostIdentity, count: Int, cause: String }
  | HostNotRetiring { host: HostIdentity, cause: String }

fn host_retirement_rows_for(host: HostIdentity) -> List<HostRetirement> {
  declared_host_retirements |> filter(r => (r.host as String) == (host as String))
}

fn host_retirement_standing(host: HostIdentity) -> HostRetirementStanding {
  host_retirement_standing_of(rows: host_retirement_rows_for(host: host), host: host)
}

// THE SELECTION RULE TAKES THE ROWS, so the ambiguous arm has an authorable red. Reading the module's
// own roster inside the decision would leave the refusal unreachable from any witness -- the rows are
// authored here and nothing outside can add a second one -- and a wall whose red cannot be written is
// a decoration that will be cited as coverage (DESIGN 4b). Supplying the population is also what
// makes this claim about ONE interface rather than about whatever the roster happens to hold today.
fn host_retirement_standing_of(rows: List<HostRetirement>, host: HostIdentity) -> HostRetirementStanding {
  if list_length(items: rows) == 0 {
    HostNotRetiring {
      host: host,
      cause: join([host as String, " carries no declared host retirement, so nothing about it may be deleted under one; a host whose admitted width refuses is a host the model cannot size, not a host somebody decided to remove"], ""),
    }
  } else if list_length(items: rows) > 1 {
    HostRetirementAmbiguous {
      host: host,
      count: list_length(items: rows),
      cause: join([
        host as String, " carries ", to_string(list_length(items: rows)),
        " declared host retirements. They may differ in search bound, captured population and local population, so which obligation a retirement route would discharge is not established -- and answering with the first would make authoring order the decision. One host is retired once or the rows are reconciled",
      ], ""),
    }
  } else {
    HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: rows.first() }
  }
}

// THE OBLIGATION, IN THE SAME SHAPE THE TRANSITION AUTHORITY PRODUCES, AND IT SAYS WHICH POPULATION
// IT CAME FROM. A retiring host owes every one of its owned slot identities from 1, because it is
// leaving entirely -- there is no surviving committed population to subtract, which is what
// distinguishes a retirement from a shrink.
//
// A CAPTURED POPULATION SUPERSEDES THE BOUND WHEN ONE EXISTS. Until then the bound is enumerated and
// the standing below reports that the set is ungrounded, so a reader is never told that an authored
// range is observed history.
type RetireeObligationBasis
  = ObligationFromCapturedSnapshot { observed_on: NonEmptyStr, units: List<NonEmptyStr> }
  | ObligationFromSearchBound { units: List<NonEmptyStr>, bound: Int, cause: String }
  | ObligationAbsent { host: HostIdentity }

// A COMPLETE CAPTURE GROUNDS THE OBLIGATION AT WHATEVER SIZE IT FOUND, INCLUDING ZERO. Anything else
// falls back to the search bound and says so; a partial capture is NOT grounded, however many members
// it carries, because the members it missed are exactly the ones an assessment would stop looking for.
// THE CAPTURE-TO-BASIS FOLD, SPLIT FROM THE ROSTER LOOKUP SO IT CAN BE EXERCISED AT ITS OWN
// INTERFACE. Reading the declared row directly left no way to hand this a complete-but-empty capture
// or a partial one, so a witness could only assert what the single declared row already produces --
// which is the supplied-inputs obligation DESIGN states: the boundary is the capture standing, and
// the claims about it supply that value instead of computing it from a roster lookup.
// THE LOCAL OBLIGATION COMES FROM THE LOCAL POPULATION, NOT FROM THE REGISTRATION BINDINGS.
//
// This mapped captured registrations through the unit projection, which on srv2's real estate would
// have produced FOUR local units against SEVEN observed trees -- and could not have done better,
// because a slot whose `.runner` is positively absent has no binding to be the image of.
fn host_retirement_obligation_basis_of(
  local_population: LocalSlotPopulation,
  host: HostIdentity,
  bound: Int,
) -> RetireeObligationBasis {
  match local_population_standing(p: local_population) {
    LocalPopulationKnown { observed_on: on, slots: _ } =>
      ObligationFromCapturedSnapshot {
        observed_on: on,
        units: local_slot_unit_obligations(p: local_population),
      }
    LocalPopulationIncomplete { cause: c } =>
      ObligationFromSearchBound {
        units: desired_unit_names_range(host: host, slot: 1, limit: bound) |> map(u => u as NonEmptyStr),
        bound: bound,
        cause: c,
      }
  }
}

fn host_retirement_local_population(host: HostIdentity) -> LocalSlotPopulation {
  match host_retirement_standing(host: host) {
    HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: r } => r.local_population
    HostRetirementAmbiguous { host: _, count: _, cause: c } => LocalPopulationUnavailable { cause: c }
    HostNotRetiring { host: h, cause: _ } =>
      LocalPopulationUnavailable {
        cause: join([h as String, " carries no declared retirement, so no local slot population is declared for it"], ""),
      }
  }
}

fn host_retirement_obligation_basis(host: HostIdentity) -> RetireeObligationBasis {
  match host_retirement_standing(host: host) {
    HostNotRetiring { host: h, cause: _ } => ObligationAbsent { host: h }
    HostRetirementAmbiguous { host: h, count: _, cause: _ } => ObligationAbsent { host: h }
    HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: r } =>
      host_retirement_obligation_basis_of(local_population: r.local_population, host: r.host, bound: r.search_bound)
  }
}

fn retiree_obligation_basis_wire(b: RetireeObligationBasis) -> String {
  match b {
    ObligationAbsent { host: _ } => "none"
    ObligationFromCapturedSnapshot { observed_on: on, units: _ } => join(["captured-snapshot observed_on=", on as String], "")
    ObligationFromSearchBound { units: _, bound: n, cause: _ } => join(["search-bound=", to_string(n), " UNGROUNDED"], "")
  }
}

// THE UNGROUNDED CAUSE, EXPORTED SO A CONSUMER CAN BLOCK ON IT RATHER THAN PRINT IT. The previous
// shape exposed only a wire string and a boolean predicate, and the production route consumed the
// wire for REPORTING while nothing consumed the predicate at all -- so an authored range reached the
// obligation consumer with a visible marker beside it and no consequence.
fn retiree_obligation_ungrounded_cause(b: RetireeObligationBasis) -> String? {
  match b {
    ObligationFromCapturedSnapshot { observed_on: _, units: _ } => none
    ObligationAbsent { host: _ } => none
    ObligationFromSearchBound { units: us, bound: n, cause: c } =>
      Present {
        value: join([
          "this host's retirement population is derived from a SEARCH BOUND of ", to_string(n),
          " rather than from an owned-identity capture, so the ", to_string(list_length(items: us)),
          " identities it names are candidates to look for and not established members: ", c,
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}

// THE OBLIGATION IS THE GROUNDED POPULATION ONLY. THE SEARCH BOUND PRODUCES NONE.
//
// The previous dataflow ran search-bound candidates straight through this function into
// `transition_required_retiring`, and from there into the route's invocations and into
// `transition_retirement_admission`'s interruption-coverage check -- so an authored range was telling
// consumers which identities OWE retirement, with the grounding blocker applied afterwards when the
// assessment was assembled. Nothing destructive followed, because permission is checked separately
// and srv2 has none; the obligation boundary was still wrong.
//
// AN EMPTY LIST HERE IS NOT THE OPPOSITE DEFECT, because the emptiness is not the whole answer: the
// basis travels beside it and says UNRESOLVED, and `grounding_blockers` turns that into a refusal. A
// consumer that read only this list and concluded "nothing to do" would be reading half the value --
// which is why `host_retirement_discovery_subjects` exists separately rather than this function
// quietly serving both purposes.
fn host_retirement_required_retirees(host: HostIdentity) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match host_retirement_obligation_basis(host: host) {
    ObligationFromCapturedSnapshot { observed_on: _, units: us } => us
    ObligationFromSearchBound { units: _, bound: _, cause: _ } => []
    ObligationAbsent { host: _ } => []
  }
}

// THE DISCOVERY SUBJECTS: identities worth LOOKING AT, carrying no claim that they exist or that
// anything is owed for them. A read-only observation over these is how the capture eventually gets
// taken; passing them where an obligation is expected is the defect this split removes.
fn host_retirement_discovery_subjects(host: HostIdentity) -> List<NonEmptyStr> {
  match host_retirement_obligation_basis(host: host) {
    ObligationFromCapturedSnapshot { observed_on: _, units: _ } => []
    ObligationAbsent { host: _ } => []
    ObligationFromSearchBound { units: us, bound: _, cause: _ } => us
  }
}

// THE DECLARED CAPTURE FOR A HOST, so a consumer classifying that host's registrations reads the
// same standing the obligation basis is derived from rather than being handed a second one.
fn host_retirement_capture(host: HostIdentity) -> CaptureCensus {
  match host_retirement_standing(host: host) {
    HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: r } => r.capture
    HostRetirementAmbiguous { host: _, count: _, cause: c } => CaptureCensusUnavailable { cause: c }
    HostNotRetiring { host: h, cause: _ } =>
      CaptureCensusUnavailable {
        cause: join([h as String, " carries no declared retirement, so no capture census is declared for it"], ""),
      }
  }
}

fn host_retirement_spec(host: HostIdentity) -> List<RunnerHostSpec> {
  match host_retirement_standing(host: host) {
    HostNotRetiring { host: _, cause: _ } => []
    HostRetirementAmbiguous { host: _, count: _, cause: _ } => []
    HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: r } => [r.spec]
  }
}


// ── OWNERSHIP IS ESTABLISHED BEFORE ANYTHING IS DELETED, AND A DECODER MISS IS NOT EVIDENCE ───
//
// `RegistrationNamesNoCommittedSlot` is the honest answer to ONE question: does this registration's
// name equal the name of a slot in the currently committed population? For a retiring host the answer
// is no for every one of its registrations, because the committed population no longer contains it.
// The decoder is right and it is being asked about the wrong thing.
//
// TWO OPPOSITE MISREADINGS OF THAT ANSWER, AND BOTH ARE REACHABLE.
//
//   AS A FILTER: "the decoder does not recognize this name, so it is not ours to worry about" -- and
//   srv2's whole GitHub estate silently leaves the obligation set at the moment its slots stop being
//   committed. The identities do not stop existing; they stop being counted, which is worse, because
//   the closure then has nothing to refuse on.
//
//   AS A LICENCE: "the decoder does not recognize this name, so it is surplus and may be deleted" --
//   and a foreign runner somebody else registered in the same organization is destroyed by our
//   convergence. `SlotConnectivityUnexpectedRegistration` exists because that class is real.
//
// SO OWNERSHIP IS ITS OWN EVIDENCE, TAKEN FROM THE NAMING AUTHORITY THAT ISSUED THE NAME. A
// registration belongs to a host when it carries that host's instance prefix -- the same forward
// naming rule `unit_is_owned_by_host` applies to units, which is where slot names come from in the
// first place. That is a positive fact about the name, not the absence of a fact about the roster.
// RECOGNITION AND OWNERSHIP ARE TWO RESULTS, AND THE FORWARD MATCH ESTABLISHES ONLY THE FIRST.
//
// The previous version compared a candidate against the names `host_registration_name_for_slot`
// generates and returned `OwnershipEstablished` on a hit. That comparison is the right repair for the
// grammar defect and it is kept -- but what it establishes is "this name DENOTES canonical slot 7",
// which is a fact about the name. "This observed registration IS OURS" is a fact about the world, and
// the function had no input carrying one: it received a host and a name, and the discovery range it
// compared against declares in its own note that it establishes nothing about what exists.
//
// THE SEPARATION IS VISIBLE IN THE DECLARED ROW TODAY. srv2's capture is Unavailable, and the old
// fold still answered `OwnershipEstablished` for `srv2-07` -- which became `SurplusOwnedRetiree` and
// entered a declared retirement's scope. The deletion gate downstream stayed shut, so nothing was
// destroyed; the STATEMENT was still stronger than its inputs, and a later consumer reading it as
// evidence would have been reading a name-shape as a provenance.
//
// SO OWNERSHIP NOW CONSUMES THE CAPTURE. A canonical name whose owner cannot be established is
// neither asserted ours nor dropped: it is RECOGNIZED AND UNRESOLVED, carrying the slot index it
// matched, so it stays accounted for while the thing that would settle it is missing. The named
// producer is L3's owned-identity capture; when it lands, the same fold answers Established without
// any new naming implementation or registry.
type RegistrationOwnership
  = OwnershipEstablished { host: HostIdentity, slot_index: Nat, observed_on: NonEmptyStr, basis: String }
  | OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved { name: NonEmptyStr, slot_index: Nat, cause: String }
  | OwnershipCandidateUnresolved { name: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }
  | OwnershipForeign { name: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

// THE DISCOVERY RANGE AND THE CANONICAL-SLOT DECISION MOVED TO `gunbc.runner_registration_capture`,
// which is where the capture context corroborates against them. Declaring them here as well would be
// two spellings of "which names does this host issue", free to disagree the first time either moved.
fn host_registration_namespace_prefix(host: HostIdentity) -> String {
  join([host as String, "-"], "")
}

// OWNERSHIP IS ESTABLISHED BY A BINDING, FOUND BY THE REGISTRATION'S OWN IDENTITY.
//
// This searched a list of captured NAMES for the candidate name. A name is the wrong key twice over:
// it is not unique across the successive incarnations of one slot, and it is not what addresses the
// remote object. The census is now searched by ORG-SCOPED ID, which is what the `.runner` record
// actually binds, and the binding it returns carries the slot the file was found under -- so
// ownership and the local unit come from one piece of evidence instead of two readings of a string.
//
// AND A PARTIAL CENSUS CAN ESTABLISH THIS MEMBER, which the previous fold refused. Its reasoning was
// that a hit inside a prefix reads the prefix as the world; that is true of the POPULATION claim and
// false of the member claim. A binding the census did observe is sound whether or not other captures
// went unread. `capture_population_standing` keeps the population question, and it still refuses.
fn capture_binding_for(
  capture: CaptureCensus,
  organization: NonEmptyStr,
  agent_id: Int,
) -> RunnerRegistrationBinding? {
  census_binding_for_agent(c: capture, organization: organization, agent_id: agent_id)
}

fn capture_unresolved_cause(capture: CaptureCensus) -> String {
  match capture {
    CaptureCensusComplete { observed_on: _, bindings: _, unresolved: _ } =>
      "the capture census is complete and holds no binding for this registration id, so this estate does not own it"
    CaptureCensusPartial { observed_on: _, bindings: _, unresolved: _, cause: c } =>
      join(["the capture census is PARTIAL (", c, "), so a registration absent from it may be in the part that was not read"], "")
    CaptureCensusUnavailable { cause: c } =>
      join(["no capture census is available (", c, ")"], "")
  }
}

// AN ID HIT DOES NOT OVERRIDE DISAGREEMENT IN THE EVIDENCE IT HIT.
//
// This decoded the candidate to slot index `i`, looked a binding up by org and id, and on a hit
// returned `OwnershipEstablished` with the slot taken FROM THE CANDIDATE NAME -- never comparing the
// binding's own host, slot or full name against it. One sound capture was enough to produce a wrong
// answer: the srv2-02 binding for id 251463, offered a candidate named
// `srv2-05-1789774588-3693107` at the same org and id, established ownership FOR SLOT 5, with a
// basis string that could simultaneously say the captured id belongs to srv2-02.
//
// THE SUBJECT NOW COMES FROM THE BINDING AND THE CANDIDATE IS CORROBORATED AGAINST IT. Three facts
// must agree -- the host, the decoded slot, and the full registration name -- and any disagreement is
// preserved as a refusal rather than resolved by preferring the candidate or the capture. Silence on
// a disagreement is the one outcome the admitted-evidence trust boundary cannot afford, because it is
// the shape a copied or mis-attributed record takes.
fn registration_ownership(
  host: HostIdentity,
  name: NonEmptyStr,
  organization: NonEmptyStr,
  agent_id: Int,
  capture: CaptureCensus,
) -> RegistrationOwnership {
  match canonical_slot_index_of(host: host, name: name) {
    Present { value: i } =>
      match capture_binding_for(capture: capture, organization: organization, agent_id: agent_id) {
        Present { value: b } =>
          if (b.host as String) != (host as String) {
            OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved {
              name: name,
              slot_index: i,
              cause: join([
                "registration id ", to_string(agent_id), " is bound by capture to host ", b.host as String,
                " and this candidate is being classified against ", host as String,
                "; an id match does not override a host disagreement",
              ], ""),
            }
          } else if (b.agent_name as String) != (name as String) {
            OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved {
              name: name,
              slot_index: i,
              cause: join([
                "registration id ", to_string(agent_id), " is bound by capture to the name ", b.agent_name as String,
                " and this candidate reports ", name as String,
                "; one remote object cannot have two identities, and neither the candidate nor the capture may be preferred",
              ], ""),
            }
          } else {
            match canonical_slot_index_of(host: host, name: b.slot_instance) {
              Absent =>
                OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved {
                  name: name,
                  slot_index: i,
                  cause: join(["the bound slot ", b.slot_instance as String, " is not a canonical slot of ", host as String, " within the discovery range"], ""),
                }
              Present { value: bound_index } =>
                if (bound_index as Int) != (i as Int) {
                  OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved {
                    name: name,
                    slot_index: i,
                    cause: join([
                      "this candidate decodes to slot ", to_string(i), " while the capture binds id ",
                      to_string(agent_id), " to ", b.slot_instance as String,
                      "; the established subject comes from the binding and the disagreement is the finding",
                    ], ""),
                  }
                } else {
                  OwnershipEstablished {
                    host: b.host,
                    slot_index: bound_index,
                    observed_on: b.provenance.observed_on,
                    basis: join([
                      "a capture taken on ", b.provenance.observed_on as String,
                      " binds registration id ", to_string(agent_id), " in ", b.organization as String,
                      " to ", b.slot_instance as String, " under ", b.capture_path as String,
                      ", and the candidate's host, decoded slot and full name all agree with it",
                    ], ""),
                  }
                }
            }
          }
        Absent =>
          OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved {
            name: name,
            slot_index: i,
            cause: join([
              name as String, " denotes ", host as String, "'s canonical slot ", to_string(i),
              ", which says what the name DENOTES and not that this estate issued the registration: ",
              capture_unresolved_cause(capture: capture),
            ], ""),
          }
      }
    Absent =>
      if starts_with(s: name as String, prefix: host_registration_namespace_prefix(host: host)) {
        OwnershipCandidateUnresolved {
          name: name,
          cause: join([name as String, " sits in ", host as String, "'s namespace but decodes to no canonical slot incarnation within the discovery range; sitting in a namespace is not evidence that we created it, and it may be neither deleted nor dropped from the assessment"], ""),
        }
      } else {
        OwnershipForeign {
          name: name,
          cause: join(["no declared host in this estate issues the registration name ", name as String, "; its owner is unestablished, and an unestablished owner authorizes neither its removal nor its silent omission"], ""),
        }
      }
  }
}

fn unit_name_for_owned_registration(name: NonEmptyStr) -> NonEmptyStr {
  runner_unit_name_of_registration(registration_name: name)
}

fn ownership_is_established(e: RegistrationOwnership) -> Bool {
  match e {
    OwnershipEstablished { host: _, slot_index: _, observed_on: _, basis: _ } => true
    OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved { name: _, slot_index: _, cause: _ } => false
    OwnershipCandidateUnresolved { name: _, cause: _ } => false
    OwnershipForeign { name: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE THIRD DISPOSITION IS THE ONE THE DECODER CANNOT EXPRESS. A committed slot is ordinary business.
// An owned identity outside the committed population is a SURPLUS RETIREE -- it stays in the
// obligation precisely because the committed-slot decoder no longer recognizes it. An unowned one
// blocks and is never touched.
//
// NOTE WHAT `SurplusOwnedRetiree` DOES NOT CARRY: permission. It says this identity is ours and is
// owed a retirement, which is the obligation half. Whether it may be DELETED is decided by
// `surplus_identity_deletable` below, which requires a declared retirement over its host as well --
// so an owned surplus identity on a host nobody retired is required to be accounted for and is still
// not deletable.
type SurplusIdentityDisposition
  = CommittedSlotIdentity { name: NonEmptyStr }
  | SurplusOwnedRetiree { name: NonEmptyStr, host: HostIdentity }
  | CanonicalOwnershipUnresolved { name: NonEmptyStr, slot_index: Nat, cause: String }
  | UnresolvedCandidate { name: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }
  | UnownedRegistration { name: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

fn surplus_identity_disposition(
  host: HostIdentity,
  decoding: RegistrationSlotDecoding,
  name: NonEmptyStr,
  organization: NonEmptyStr,
  agent_id: Int,
  capture: CaptureCensus,
) -> SurplusIdentityDisposition {
  match decoding {
    RegistrationNamesCommittedSlot { slot: _ } => CommittedSlotIdentity { name: name }
    RegistrationNamesNoCommittedSlot { registration_name: rn } =>
      match registration_ownership(host: host, name: rn, organization: organization, agent_id: agent_id, capture: capture) {
        OwnershipEstablished { host: h, slot_index: _, observed_on: _, basis: _ } => SurplusOwnedRetiree { name: rn, host: h }
        OwnershipCanonicalUnresolved { name: n, slot_index: i, cause: c } =>
          CanonicalOwnershipUnresolved { name: n, slot_index: i, cause: c }
        OwnershipCandidateUnresolved { name: n, cause: c } => UnresolvedCandidate { name: n, cause: c }
        OwnershipForeign { name: n, cause: c } => UnownedRegistration { name: n, cause: c }
      }
  }
}

// THIS IS SCOPE, NOT A DELETION AUTHORIZATION, AND THE NAME SAYS SO BECAUSE THE PREVIOUS ONE DID NOT.
//
// It was called `surplus_identity_deletable` and answered true from ownership plus a declared
// retirement. Both facts are necessary and neither is sufficient: nothing here consults the
// retirement POSTCONDITION for the identity, and nothing here holds the authorization to write to
// GitHub or to the host. A second predicate that answers "deletable" beside the real teardown
// admission is a competing authorization, and the one that disagrees quietly is the one that never
// runs.
//
// WHAT IT ACTUALLY ESTABLISHES: this identity falls inside the scope of a declared retirement, so it
// is a subject this transaction is entitled to reason about. Final deletion admission stays where it
// already lives -- `slot_removal_realization`'s `RemovalTeardownAuthorized`, which requires an exact
// `RetirementComplete` for the unit and the operator's interruption coverage.
fn surplus_identity_within_retirement_scope(d: SurplusIdentityDisposition) -> Bool {
  match d {
    CommittedSlotIdentity { name: _ } => false
    CanonicalOwnershipUnresolved { name: _, slot_index: _, cause: _ } => false
    UnresolvedCandidate { name: _, cause: _ } => false
    UnownedRegistration { name: _, cause: _ } => false
    SurplusOwnedRetiree { name: _, host: h } =>
      match host_retirement_standing(host: h) {
        HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: _ } => true
        HostRetirementAmbiguous { host: _, count: _, cause: _ } => false
        HostNotRetiring { host: _, cause: _ } => false
      }
  }
}

// AN IDENTITY THAT MAY NOT BE DELETED IS STILL AN IDENTITY THE CLOSURE MUST ACCOUNT FOR. This is the
// half the filter reading loses: `surplus_identity_deletable` answering false is not permission to
// stop looking.
fn surplus_identity_blocks_closure(d: SurplusIdentityDisposition) -> Bool {
  match d {
    CommittedSlotIdentity { name: _ } => false
    CanonicalOwnershipUnresolved { name: _, slot_index: _, cause: _ } => true
    UnresolvedCandidate { name: _, cause: _ } => true
    UnownedRegistration { name: _, cause: _ } => true
    SurplusOwnedRetiree { name: _, host: _ } => true
  }
}

fn surplus_identity_name(d: SurplusIdentityDisposition) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match d {
    CommittedSlotIdentity { name: n } => n
    CanonicalOwnershipUnresolved { name: n, slot_index: _, cause: _ } => n
    UnresolvedCandidate { name: n, cause: _ } => n
    UnownedRegistration { name: n, cause: _ } => n
    SurplusOwnedRetiree { name: n, host: _ } => n
  }
}


// ── SLOT RETIREMENT SATISFIED IS NOT ESTATE CLEANUP ───────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `RetirementComplete` is a statement about ONE systemd unit: replacement is inhibited, the service
// is not running, and its workload cgroup is quiescent. Three further things survive that state, each
// with a different owner and a different remedy, and collapsing them into the slot standing is how a
// host reports itself retired while its registrations still appear in the organization roster and a
// timer on the machine is still trying to bring its runners back.
//
//   REGISTRATION REMOVAL -- the GitHub side. A masked unit does not delist anything; the org roster
//   is remote state reached through the App, and its removal is a separate authorized write with its
//   own readback.
//
//   RUNNER TREE REMOVAL -- the on-disk `_work` and runner directories. `slot_removal_realization`
//   authorizes this per identity and only against an exact `RetirementComplete`, which is the
//   ordering, not the discharge.
//
//   LIVENESS RECONCILE TIMER RETIRED -- the one that undoes the other two. It is modelled below.
//
// THE FOLD REQUIRES ALL OF THEM, so `EstateCleanupComplete` has no arm reachable from slot standings
// alone. That is the point: the invalid state is "a retirement reported satisfied on slot evidence",
// and it stops being writable rather than being caught by a reviewer noticing the difference.
type EstateObligation
  = RegistrationRemoval
  | RunnerTreeRemoval
  | LivenessReconcileTimerRetired

fn estate_obligation_wire(o: EstateObligation) -> String {
  match o {
    RegistrationRemoval => "registration-removal"
    RunnerTreeRemoval => "runner-tree-removal"
    LivenessReconcileTimerRetired => "liveness-reconcile-timer-retired"
  }
}

data host_retirement_estate_obligations: List<EstateObligation> = [RegistrationRemoval, RunnerTreeRemoval, LivenessReconcileTimerRetired]

// DISCHARGE IS EVIDENCE-SHAPED AND HAS THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO. An obligation nobody could observe is
// not an obligation that is outstanding-because-we-looked; the difference decides whether the remedy
// is to act or to restore a reading.
type EstateObligationStanding
  = EstateObligationDischarged { obligation: EstateObligation, evidence: String }
  | EstateObligationOutstanding { obligation: EstateObligation, cause: String }
  | EstateObligationUnobserved { obligation: EstateObligation, cause: String }

fn estate_standing_obligation(s: EstateObligationStanding) -> EstateObligation {
  match s {
    EstateObligationDischarged { obligation: o, evidence: _ } => o
    EstateObligationOutstanding { obligation: o, cause: _ } => o
    EstateObligationUnobserved { obligation: o, cause: _ } => o
  }
}

fn estate_standing_discharged(s: EstateObligationStanding) -> Bool {
  match s {
    EstateObligationDischarged { obligation: _, evidence: _ } => true
    EstateObligationOutstanding { obligation: _, cause: _ } => false
    EstateObligationUnobserved { obligation: _, cause: _ } => false
  }
}

type EstateCleanupStanding
  = EstateCleanupComplete
  | EstateCleanupOutstanding { outstanding: List<EstateObligation> }

// ONE AUTHORITATIVE READING PER OBLIGATION, AND A CONTRADICTION IS A REFUSAL RATHER THAN A SEARCH.
//
// The previous fold filtered the standings to the DISCHARGED ones and then asked whether each
// obligation appeared among them. That is "any success exists", and it discards every disagreeing
// reading: three discharged obligations plus an `EstateObligationUnobserved` for one of those same
// three still produced `EstateCleanupComplete`, because the fold never looked outside the subset it
// had already filtered to.
//
// A subject with two readings has not been observed twice; it has been observed inconsistently, and
// the honest answer is that neither reading is authoritative. Selecting one -- first, last, or the
// convenient one -- is an unresolved selection rule wearing a decision's clothes.
fn estate_standings_for(standings: List<EstateObligationStanding>, o: EstateObligation) -> List<EstateObligationStanding> {
  standings |> filter(s => estate_obligation_wire(o: estate_standing_obligation(s: s)) == estate_obligation_wire(o: o))
}

fn estate_obligation_is_settled(standings: List<EstateObligationStanding>, o: EstateObligation) -> Bool {
  let mine = estate_standings_for(standings: standings, o: o)
  list_length(items: mine) == 1 && all(mine, s => estate_standing_discharged(s: s))
}

fn estate_cleanup_standing(standings: List<EstateObligationStanding>) -> EstateCleanupStanding {
  let outstanding = host_retirement_estate_obligations
    |> filter(o => !estate_obligation_is_settled(standings: standings, o: o))
  if list_length(items: outstanding) == 0 {
    EstateCleanupComplete
  } else {
    EstateCleanupOutstanding { outstanding: outstanding }
  }
}


// MASKING A UNIT WHOSE FRAGMENT LIVES IN /etc/systemd/system NEEDS THE FRAGMENT MOVED FIRST.
//
// `mask` works by creating a symlink to /dev/null at the unit's name in /etc/systemd/system. If a
// REAL unit file already occupies that path, systemd refuses rather than replacing it, so the
// actuation fails on exactly the units most likely to need it -- anything installed by hand or by an
// out-of-band installer, which is what the reconcile mechanism is.
//
// MEASURED ON srv2, 2026-09-19: masking runner-liveness-reconcile.timer and .service refused until
// the fragments were moved to /var/lib/gunbc-retired/20260919/ with their digests preserved, after
// which both read masked and `systemctl start` was refused as masked.
//
// THIS IS A PRECONDITION OF THE ACTUATION, NOT A SEPARATE DECISION, and it is typed here so a
// retirement that meets it is distinguishable from one that has not looked. What this module does NOT
// do is perform the move: relocating a fragment is an effect, and every effect in this lane is
// executed by the reviewed plan/apply artifact rather than by an observer. A fragment in the
// managed-unit directory therefore reports RELOCATION REQUIRED with the path that must move, so the
// plan renders it before the mask instead of the mask failing at apply time.
data systemd_managed_unit_dir: NonEmptyStr = "/etc/systemd/system" as NonEmptyStr

type MaskFragmentPrecondition
  = MaskFragmentClear { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | MaskFragmentRelocationRequired { unit: NonEmptyStr, fragment_path: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }
  | MaskFragmentUnobserved { unit: NonEmptyStr, cause: String }

fn mask_fragment_precondition(unit: NonEmptyStr, fragment_path: ReconcilePropertyReading) -> MaskFragmentPrecondition {
  match fragment_path {
    ReconcilePropertyUnreadable { cause: c } =>
      MaskFragmentUnobserved {
        unit: unit,
        cause: join(["the FragmentPath of ", unit as String, " could not be read (", c, "), so whether masking it needs a relocation is unknown"], ""),
      }
    ReconcilePropertyValue { value: p } =>
      if trim(s: p) == "" {
        MaskFragmentClear { unit: unit }
      } else if starts_with(s: trim(s: p), prefix: join([systemd_managed_unit_dir as String, "/"], "")) {
        MaskFragmentRelocationRequired {
          unit: unit,
          fragment_path: trim(s: p) as NonEmptyStr,
          cause: join([
            "mask creates a symlink at this unit's name under ", systemd_managed_unit_dir as String,
            " and refuses rather than replacing the real file already there (", trim(s: p),
            "); the fragment must be relocated, with its digest preserved, before the mask can take",
          ], ""),
        }
      } else {
        MaskFragmentClear { unit: unit }
      }
  }
}

fn mask_fragment_precondition_wire(p: MaskFragmentPrecondition) -> String {
  match p {
    MaskFragmentClear { unit: _ } => "clear"
    MaskFragmentRelocationRequired { unit: _, fragment_path: fp, cause: _ } =>
      join(["RELOCATION-REQUIRED ", fp as String], "")
    MaskFragmentUnobserved { unit: _, cause: _ } => "UNOBSERVED"
  }
}

// ── THE RECONCILE TIMER'S RETIRED INTENT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `runner-liveness-reconcile.timer` runs on the runner hosts every two minutes and its service
// restores runner liveness -- that is its whole job, and on a host that is still in the estate it is
// doing the right thing. On a RETIRING host it is the mechanism that undoes the retirement: every
// masked slot it can restart, every registration it can re-mint, it will.
//
// DISABLING IT BY HAND IS NOT ENOUGH, AND THE REASON IS THE SECOND HALF OF THIS SECTION'S TITLE. A
// hand-disabled timer is a fact about the host at one instant; the next convergence pass compares the
// host to the MODEL, and a model that says nothing about this timer says "active" by inheritance from
// every other host. So the manual retirement is reverted by a legitimate pass doing exactly what it
// was asked to do, and the no-change pass that follows reports convergence over a state nobody
// intended.
//
// THE INTENT IS THEREFORE DERIVED FROM THE RETIREMENT ROSTER RATHER THAN AUTHORED BESIDE IT. There is
// no second list of hosts-whose-timer-is-off to fall out of step with the list of hosts that are
// retiring: declaring the retirement IS declaring the intent, and a host removed from the roster gets
// its timer back by the same act.
data runner_liveness_reconcile_timer_unit: NonEmptyStr = "runner-liveness-reconcile.timer" as NonEmptyStr

// THE SERVICE THE TIMER STARTS, NAMED BESIDE IT RATHER THAN DERIVED BY STRING SURGERY ON THE TIMER.
// systemd's default pairing is the same stem with a .service suffix, and both names are written here
// so a reader sees the pair rather than inferring one from the other at a call site.
data runner_liveness_reconcile_service_unit: NonEmptyStr = "runner-liveness-reconcile.service" as NonEmptyStr

type ReconcileTimerIntent
  = ReconcileTimerActive { host: HostIdentity }
  | ReconcileTimerRetired { host: HostIdentity, unit: NonEmptyStr, reason: NonEmptyStr }

fn runner_liveness_reconcile_timer_intent(host: HostIdentity) -> ReconcileTimerIntent {
  match host_retirement_standing(host: host) {
    HostNotRetiring { host: h, cause: _ } => ReconcileTimerActive { host: h }
    HostRetirementAmbiguous { host: h, count: _, cause: _ } => ReconcileTimerActive { host: h }
    HostRetirementDeclared { retirement: r } =>
      ReconcileTimerRetired {
        host: r.host,
        unit: runner_liveness_reconcile_timer_unit,
        reason: r.operator_reason,
      }
  }
}

fn reconcile_timer_intent_wire(i: ReconcileTimerIntent) -> String {
  match i {
    ReconcileTimerActive { host: _ } => "active"
    ReconcileTimerRetired { host: _, unit: _, reason: _ } => "retired"
  }
}

// THE OBSERVED SIDE, JOINED TO THE INTENT. A timer that is enabled on a host whose intent is Retired
// is the exact regression this section exists to catch, and it is reported as an OUTSTANDING estate
// obligation rather than as a converged difference -- a retirement is not satisfied while the
// mechanism that reverses it is armed.
// DISABLING A UNIT DOES NOT STOP IT, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS DISCHARGED ON EXACTLY THAT.
//
// It read one property, `UnitFileState`, and treated everything that was not `enabled` or
// `enabled-runtime` as retired -- including a blank string and any spelling it did not recognize.
// systemd documents the inverse case plainly: `disable` removes the enablement symlinks and does not
// stop a running unit. So a timer that had been disabled while still active discharged the
// obligation, and so did a reading nobody could parse.
//
// AND ENABLEMENT IS NOT THE WHOLE MECHANISM ANYWAY. A reconcile timer that is inhibited and inactive
// still leaves the question the obligation is actually about: whether the SERVICE it triggers is
// still running, because an invocation already in flight goes on restoring runners after its timer is
// gone. That is the same unit-versus-workload separation the slot observer makes for cgroups, and it
// was missing here.
//
// THREE INDEPENDENT FACTS, AND THE DISCHARGE IS THEIR CONJUNCTION:
//
//   PERSISTENT INHIBITION -- UnitFileState. `masked` or `disabled` means the manager will not arm it
//                            again; anything unrecognized is unobserved, never off.
//   TIMER ACTIVITY        -- ActiveState of the timer. Inactive or absent; `active` means it is still
//                            armed for its next elapse whatever its enablement says.
//   TRIGGERED RECONCILER  -- ActiveState of the service the timer starts. A reconcile invocation that
//                            is still running can restore the population this retirement removes.
//
// EVERY READING IS THREE-VALUED, so a property that could not be read blocks rather than defaulting.
type ReconcileMechanismReading {
  timer_load_state: ReconcilePropertyReading
  timer_unit_file_state: ReconcilePropertyReading
  timer_active_state: ReconcilePropertyReading
  service_active_state: ReconcilePropertyReading
  service_unit_file_state: ReconcilePropertyReading
}

type ReconcilePropertyReading
  = ReconcilePropertyValue { value: String }
  | ReconcilePropertyUnreadable { cause: String }

// THE ADMITTED INHIBITED SPELLINGS ARE ENUMERATED, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS UNKNOWN. An allowlist of
// states that mean "will not be armed again" fails closed on a spelling systemd adds later; a denylist
// of states that mean "armed" fails open on exactly the same change.
// THE UNIT-STATE VOCABULARY IS UPSTREAM'S AND IS CONSUMED, NOT RE-MINTED.
//
// These predicates were bare string comparisons -- "active" || "activating" || "deactivating" ||
// "reloading" for running, "inactive" || "failed" beside it for known, "not-found" for absence, and a
// hand-spelled list of enablement words. Every one of those spellings is already declared:
// `extdeps.systemd` owns `SystemdUnitActiveState` and `SystemdUnitLoadState` with their parsers, and
// `extdeps.systemd.systemctl` owns `UnitFileState` with `parse_unit_file_state`. A second spelling of a cited upstream is the fork DESIGN section 3
// forbids, and the tell was in this file already: the route reaches those properties through the
// modeled `SystemdUnitProperty` for the property NAMES and then dropped to literals for the VALUES.
//
// THE PARSERS' ABSENT ARM IS THE THREE-VALUED READING THESE FOLDS HAND-ROLLED. `none` means systemd
// said a word this model does not know, which is exactly the unknown that must not become a default --
// so consuming the authority replaces both the spelling list AND the is-known predicate beside it.
fn reconcile_active_state_is_running(state: String) -> Bool {
  match parse_systemd_unit_active_state(raw: state) {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: s } =>
      match s {
        Active => true
        Activating => true
        Deactivating => true
        Reloading => true
        Inactive => false
        Failed => false
      }
  }
}

fn reconcile_active_state_is_known(state: String) -> Bool {
  match parse_systemd_unit_active_state(raw: state) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

// A RUNTIME MASK IS NOT A PERSISTENT INHIBITOR, AND ACCEPTING ONE DISCHARGED THIS OBLIGATION ON A
// FENCE THAT ENDS AT THE NEXT REBOOT. systemd documents `mask --runtime` as lasting until reboot;
// this obligation is that the reconcile mechanism cannot come back and restore the population the
// retirement removed, and a fence the next boot dissolves does not establish it. `UnitMaskedRuntime`
// is therefore a KNOWN state that does not inhibit -- the reading is legible, it simply is not the
// answer the obligation needs.
// ONLY A MASK INHIBITS. `disabled` WAS ACCEPTED HERE AND IS NOT ENOUGH.
//
// `disable` removes the [Install] symlinks, so the unit will not be armed at boot -- which is what
// made it look like inhibition. It does not refuse ACTIVATION: a disabled unit still starts on an
// explicit `systemctl start`, and still starts when another unit pulls it in through Wants= or
// Requires=. `mask` links the unit name to /dev/null, which refuses activation whatever requests it.
//
// THE OBLIGATION IS NOT "WILL IT COME BACK BY ITSELF", IT IS "CAN IT COME BACK". A reconciler that
// restores the population this retirement is removing defeats the retirement whether it was armed by
// a timer, by a dependency, or by a hand at a terminal, so a fence that stops only the first is not
// the fence this obligation names. Accepting `disabled` let the weaker act discharge the stronger
// claim -- the shape this lane has been finding all day, one seam further in.
fn reconcile_unit_file_state_inhibits(state: String) -> Bool {
  match parse_unit_file_state(raw: state) {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: s } =>
      match s {
        UnitMasked => true
        UnitDisabled => false
        UnitMaskedRuntime => false
        UnitEnabled => false
        UnitEnabledRuntime => false
        UnitLinked => false
        UnitStatic => false
        UnitIndirect => false
        UnitGenerated => false
        UnitTransient => false
        UnitBad => false
      }
  }
}

fn reconcile_unit_file_state_is_known(state: String) -> Bool {
  match parse_unit_file_state(raw: state) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

fn reconcile_load_state_is_absent(state: String) -> Bool {
  match parse_systemd_unit_load_state(raw: state) {
    Present { value: LoadStateNotFound } => true
    Present { value: LoadStateLoaded } => false
    Present { value: LoadStateBadSetting } => false
    Present { value: LoadStateError } => false
    Present { value: LoadStateMasked } => false
    Absent => false
  }
}

fn reconcile_load_state_is_known(state: String) -> Bool {
  match parse_systemd_unit_load_state(raw: state) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

fn reconcile_property_value(r: ReconcilePropertyReading) -> String {
  match r {
    ReconcilePropertyValue { value: v } => v
    ReconcilePropertyUnreadable { cause: _ } => ""
  }
}

fn reconcile_property_is_readable(r: ReconcilePropertyReading) -> Bool {
  match r {
    ReconcilePropertyValue { value: _ } => true
    ReconcilePropertyUnreadable { cause: _ } => false
  }
}

fn reconcile_reading_text(reading: ReconcileMechanismReading) -> String {
  join([
    "LoadState=", reconcile_reading_field_text(r: reading.timer_load_state),
    " UnitFileState=", reconcile_reading_field_text(r: reading.timer_unit_file_state),
    " timer.ActiveState=", reconcile_reading_field_text(r: reading.timer_active_state),
    " service.ActiveState=", reconcile_reading_field_text(r: reading.service_active_state),
    " service.UnitFileState=", reconcile_reading_field_text(r: reading.service_unit_file_state),
  ], "")
}

fn reconcile_reading_field_text(r: ReconcilePropertyReading) -> String {
  match r {
    ReconcilePropertyValue { value: v } => if v == "" { "<blank>" } else { v }
    ReconcilePropertyUnreadable { cause: c } => join(["<unreadable: ", c, ">"], "")
  }
}

// THE FOLD. Unobserved comes first and covers both a refused read and a value this model does not
// recognize, because "I do not know what systemd just told me" and "I could not ask" have the same
// consequence and the opposite of the one a default would give them.
//
// BOTH UNITS' RUNTIME STANDING IS ESTABLISHED BEFORE ANY ABSENCE BRANCH, AND THAT ORDER IS THE
// SECOND REPAIR HERE. An
// earlier cut discharged on `LoadState=not-found` immediately, before looking at either activity
// value -- so `not-found` beside an ACTIVE reconciler discharged, and so did `not-found` beside a
// blank one. A timer and the service it activates are distinct units; the absence of the timer
// answers nothing about the service, and an invocation already in flight goes on restoring runners
// whatever happened to the thing that started it.
//
// AND THE SAME ORTHOGONALITY APPLIES TO THE TIMER ITSELF, which a first correction missed by moving
// only the service checks up. systemd documents `LoadState` and `ActiveState` as independent: a unit
// whose configuration is not loaded may still be RUNNING. So `not-found` beside `ActiveState=active`
// discharged, and so did `activating`, `deactivating` and `reloading`. A load state is an observation
// about configuration, never proof of runtime inactivity. Both units now have their activity
// established -- known, and not running -- before absence is consulted at all, so no positive
// activity reading can be overridden by it.
fn reconcile_timer_estate_standing(
  intent: ReconcileTimerIntent,
  reading: ReconcileMechanismReading,
) -> EstateObligationStanding {
  match intent {
    ReconcileTimerActive { host: h } =>
      EstateObligationOutstanding {
        obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
        cause: join([h as String, " carries no declared retirement, so its reconcile timer is intended ACTIVE. This fold refuses to discharge rather than answering that the obligation is met, because a discharged reconcile-timer obligation records a RETIRED timer, and recording one against a host still in the estate would be a false fact about a machine that needs its reconciliation running"], ""),
      }
    ReconcileTimerRetired { host: h, unit: u, reason: _ } => {
      let file_state = reconcile_property_value(r: reading.timer_unit_file_state)
      let timer_active = reconcile_property_value(r: reading.timer_active_state)
      let service_active = reconcile_property_value(r: reading.service_active_state)
      if !reconcile_property_is_readable(r: reading.timer_load_state)
        || !reconcile_property_is_readable(r: reading.timer_unit_file_state)
        || !reconcile_property_is_readable(r: reading.timer_active_state)
        || !reconcile_property_is_readable(r: reading.service_active_state)
        || !reconcile_property_is_readable(r: reading.service_unit_file_state) {
        EstateObligationUnobserved {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join(["the reconcile mechanism on ", h as String, " could not be fully read (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "), so whether it can still restore runners is unknown"], ""),
        }
      } else if !reconcile_load_state_is_known(state: reconcile_property_value(r: reading.timer_load_state)) {
        EstateObligationUnobserved {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join(["the reconcile timer on ", h as String, " reported a load state this model does not recognize (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "); an unrecognized state is unknown, not absent"], ""),
        }
      } else if !reconcile_active_state_is_known(state: service_active) {
        EstateObligationUnobserved {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join(["the reconcile SERVICE on ", h as String, " reported an activity state this model does not recognize or cannot read (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "); the service is a separate unit from its timer and its own state must be established"], ""),
        }
      } else if reconcile_active_state_is_running(state: service_active) {
        EstateObligationOutstanding {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join(["the reconcile service on ", h as String, " is still running (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "); an invocation already in flight goes on restoring runners whatever has happened to the timer that started it"], ""),
        }
      } else if !reconcile_active_state_is_known(state: timer_active) {
        EstateObligationUnobserved {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join(["the reconcile TIMER on ", h as String, " reported an activity state this model does not recognize or cannot read (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "); a configuration that was not found does not make the unit's runtime state known"], ""),
        }
      } else if reconcile_active_state_is_running(state: timer_active) {
        EstateObligationOutstanding {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join([u as String, " on ", h as String, " is still ACTIVE (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "); LoadState and ActiveState are orthogonal -- a unit whose configuration is not loaded may still be running -- and an armed timer elapses whatever its load or enablement says"], ""),
        }
      } else if !reconcile_unit_file_state_is_known(state: reconcile_property_value(r: reading.service_unit_file_state)) {
        EstateObligationUnobserved {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join(["the reconcile SERVICE on ", h as String, " reported a configuration state this model does not recognize (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "); the service is a separate unit and its own inhibition must be established, not inferred from the timer's"], ""),
        }
      } else if !reconcile_unit_file_state_inhibits(state: reconcile_property_value(r: reading.service_unit_file_state)) {
        EstateObligationOutstanding {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join([
            "the reconcile SERVICE on ", h as String, " is not masked (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading),
            "). Retiring the timer stops the schedule and leaves the reconciler itself startable: masking applies to the NAMED unit, so a masked timer beside a disabled service still admits an explicit `systemctl start` of the service and still admits another unit pulling it in. An inactive reading is not evidence of inhibition -- it is evidence about this instant -- and the reconciler is what restores the population this retirement removes",
          ], ""),
        }
      } else if reconcile_load_state_is_absent(state: reconcile_property_value(r: reading.timer_load_state)) {
        EstateObligationDischarged {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          evidence: join([u as String, " on ", h as String, " has no loaded configuration, is established not running, and its reconcile service is established not running AND masked (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), ")"], ""),
        }
      } else if !reconcile_unit_file_state_is_known(state: file_state) {
        EstateObligationUnobserved {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join(["the reconcile mechanism on ", h as String, " reported a state this model does not recognize (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), "); an unrecognized state is unknown, not off"], ""),
        }
      } else if !reconcile_unit_file_state_inhibits(state: file_state) {
        EstateObligationOutstanding {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          cause: join([
            u as String, " on ", h as String, " is not masked (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading),
            "). A disabled unit is not inhibited: it still starts on an explicit `systemctl start` and still starts when another unit pulls it in through Wants= or Requires=, and either restores the population this retirement removes",
          ], ""),
        }
      } else {
        EstateObligationDischarged {
          obligation: LivenessReconcileTimerRetired,
          evidence: join([u as String, " on ", h as String, " is inhibited and inactive and its triggered reconciler is both masked and not running (", reconcile_reading_text(reading: reading), ") against a declared retired intent"], ""),
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
